  <p>This is not a haunted house film. The Woman standing in Ramona's yard is a personification of death, and the film is about grief, guilt, and the cost of surviving when others did not. The traditional score reflects a film that takes mortality and family seriously, positions maternal sacrifice as genuine heroism, and does not use its horror framework to make political points. Blumhouse produced it lean and effective.</p>
